how to do the connection from ec2 to your localmachince

- open the ec2dashboard
- create an ec2 instance
- while creating an instance .pem file will be downloaded save the file
- click your instance and select connect instance
- select the ssh client and futher steps will be displayed there
-open an SSH client.
-Locate your private key file in the present working place. The key used to launch this instance is sofi.pem
-Run this command, if necessary, to ensure your key is not publicly viewable.
 #chmod 400 sofi.pem
-Connect to your instance using its Public DNS:ec2-18-116-199-158.us-east-2.compute.amazonaws.com
- Example:
 #ssh -i "sofi.pem" ec2-user@ec2-18-116-199-158.us-east-2.compute.amazonaws.com



how to create an instance in ec2 using terraform

- install the terraform in your CLI
- #curl -fsSL https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo apt-key add -
- #sudo apt-add-repository "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main"
-#sudo apt install terraform
-after installing the terraform
-open an aws account
-select security on the top and go to access creditals option
- create a access key and save the file. (Two keys are provided)
- switch to the terminal
-#cd ~
#mkdir project-terraform
#cd project-terraform
# vim create_ec2.tf
# vim aws.tf
- to execte the file
#terraform init
#terraform plan
#terraform apply
it will ask to enter a value
Enter a value: yes
- to list the resources created by terraform
#terraform state list



How to push the code on github
-Create a new repo with below name, make sure it is a private repo. Also do not click on initialize this repository with a README option.
-Note down the remote url as highligted 
-Note:
-If you have any issues in uploading tf files, you may not have created ssh-keys and uploaded into GitHub. Create ssh keys using ssh-keygen command:

ssh-keygen
This should generate both public and private keys.
Copy the public keys by executing the below command:
sudo cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

Initialize the directory first
git init

The above command will create local git repository.
Now add terraform files.
git add *.tf

git commit -m "Added terraform files"
Copy the below red highlighted url from above screenshots circled in red.
git remote add origin your remote repo url per above screenshot

Now push the code into GitHub
git push -u origin master

Now Login to GitHub to view the Terraform files

You may get this error if you have not uploaded ssh keys into GitHub/BitBucket.


